cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use the DEMES model software or the NLU ideas presented here in your work, please cite it as follows."
type: software
title: "DEMES: Deconstructive Encoding of Meaning and Expressive Syntax"
abstract: >
  DEMES is a project with the aim of developing a differentiable neural symbolic reasoning framework for natural language processing (NLP) and 
  natural language understanding (NLU) tasks. The framework integrates symbolic reasoning capabilities with neural network architectures, enabling 
  more interpretable and robust AI systems. The English language is encoded into a set of word primitives that constitute mathematical changes in a World Model, 
  solving Harnad's Symbol Grounding Problem by encoding meaning not as a circular definition referencing other words, but as mathematical changes in a system. This 
  is what we deem to be "Deconstructive Encoding of Meaning and Expressive Syntax" (DEMES). We only utilize free, locally-run, open-source LLMs for input and output 
  formatting to avoid rigid responses and capture uncertain or ambiguous language. Due to the mathematical encoding of meaning, DEMES is able to handle the English language 
  without any risk of hallucinations.
